The neutral product is **cis-$[\mathrm{NiCl_2(dppe)}]$**, and the displaced material is **six <waters> per <nickel>**:
$$
\mathrm{NiCl_2\cdot6H_2O+dppe\longrightarrow[NiCl_2(dppe)]+6H_2O}.
$$
The two <phosphorus> donors of <dppe> chelate the same <metal>, forcing adjacent donor positions. The <diamagnetism> identifies a square-planar, paired $d^8$ Ni(II) centre rather than a tetrahedral high-spin $d^8$ centre. Its valence count is $8+4_{\rm P,P}+4_{\rm Cl,Cl}=16$ <electrons>. The reaction is <ligand substitution>, not reduction of Ni(II).

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The relevant chelate rings contain **four atoms for <dppm>, five for <dppe>, and six for <dppp>**, counting the <nickel> and both <phosphorus> atoms. The one-carbon bridge in <dppm> forces a compact ring and the small <bite angle> $75.62^\circ$. A two-carbon tether accommodates a five-membered chelate closer to the preferred square-planar cis angle, giving $86.99^\circ$. The more flexible three-carbon tether can open slightly beyond $90^\circ$, giving $91.77^\circ$.

For a simplified symmetric chelate with Ni–P distance $r$ and P–P donor separation $d$,
$$
d=2r\sin(\beta/2).
$$
Increasing the accessible donor separation therefore increases the <bite angle> $\beta$. The ligand is a three-dimensional tether, so ring puckering and substituent crowding matter as well as the number of methylene groups. This geometrical explanation does not require different formal <oxidation states> or <electron> counts for the three complexes.

