First hydrolyse both Si–Cl groups to give a <silanol> of <monomer functionality> two, then condense Si–OH groups to form the Si–O–Si backbone:
$$
\mathrm{R_2SiCl_2+2H_2O\longrightarrow R_2Si(OH)_2+2HCl},
$$
$$
\mathrm{{\equiv}Si{-}OH+HO{-}Si{\equiv}\longrightarrow{\equiv}Si{-}O{-}Si{\equiv}+H_2O}.
$$
**<monomer functionality> two favours chains, with cyclic oligomers also possible.** Removal of condensation products or equilibration under appropriate conditions favours high <molar mass>. The <hydrolysis> and condensation can overlap in a one-pot process; the equations distinguish their chemical functions rather than requiring isolation of every silanediol.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**R$_3$SiCl acts as a monofunctional <chain termination> reagent.** <hydrolysis> gives R$_3$SiOH, which forms a terminal R$_3$Si–O unit but cannot continue growth through a second <silanol> group. Increasing its proportion therefore increases the number of capped ends and lowers the attainable average chain length. The resulting material generally has lower viscosity and less chain entanglement, rather than acquiring a cross-linked network merely from end-capping.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**RSiCl$_3$ introduces branching and, at sufficient conversion and loading, <cross-links>.** <hydrolysis> gives RSi(OH)$_3$, with three possible siloxane connections per <silicon>. Incorporation links chains into a network, suppressing flow and usually increasing stiffness and dimensional retention. A sufficiently connected permanent network behaves as a <thermoset> or cross-linked <elastomer> rather than an ordinary flowing linear <polymer>. A small concentration need not immediately produce a macroscopic gel; conversion and network connectivity matter.

= c
{parent=B9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Prepare a chlorophosphazene ring from <phosphorus pentachloride> and <ammonium chloride>, isolate the cyclic trimer, open the rings to make the linear chloropolymer, and finally replace P–Cl by P–OEt:
$$
\mathrm{3PCl_5+3NH_4Cl\longrightarrow(NPCl_2)_3+12HCl},
$$
$$
\mathrm{m(NPCl_2)_3\xrightarrow{\text{controlled heating}}[NPCl_2]_{3m}},
$$
$$
\mathrm{[NPCl_2]_n+2nNaOEt\longrightarrow[NP(OEt)_2]_n+2nNaCl}.
$$
The first stage can produce a mixture of cyclic oligomers, so isolation of <hexachlorocyclotriphosphazene> is important. Controlled thermal <ring-opening polymerization>, conventionally around $250\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ for the classical route, gives soluble linear <polydichlorophosphazene> before excessive cross-linking. Moisture exclusion avoids uncontrolled <hydrolysis> of the reactive P–Cl precursor.

The target is <poly-bis-ethoxy-phosphazene>. The final step is <nucleophilic substitution> with an <ethoxide> salt in a compatible dry medium, replacing two <chlorides> per <phosphorus>. **Polymerize the chlorinated ring first, then substitute the linear <polymer>**: complete ethoxylation of the isolated ring does not by itself produce the requested high <polymer>. The primary macromolecular-substitution work explicitly includes the ethoxy derivative.<Phosphonitrilic Compounds. VI. High Molecular Weight Poly(alkoxy- and aryloxyphosphazenes) by Harry R. Allcock and others>[Inorganic Chemistry 5, pp. 1709–1715; alkoxide substitution of linear polydichlorophosphazene, including ethoxy derivatives]{ref}

= d
{parent=B9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**The chemical requirement for permanence is retention of the backbone and network in a wet environment.** A suitably prepared <polysiloxane> has strong Si–O bonding, relatively slow backbone cleavage under mild aqueous conditions, and flexible chains that can be retained in a cross-linked elastic network. In the comparison intended here, <hydrolysis> of susceptible <phosphazene> substituents and P–N linkages leads to <chain scission>, reduced <molar mass> and loss of mechanical integrity, which conflicts with permanence.

This is a comparison of chemical durability, not a universal statement that every <polyphosphazene> degrades rapidly or that every <polysiloxane> is indefinitely inert. <phosphazene> side groups can strongly change access of <water> and <hydrolysis> rates; siloxanes can also be cleaved under sufficiently acidic or basic conditions. Those structural and kinetic qualifications matter even before any biological suitability is considered.

